## What a `type: content-type` instance is

A **single-axis pointer** characterizing one dimension of external-content shape — either a **format** (MIME type) or a **purpose** (kind), never both.  Sibling of [`resource`](resource.md) under the parent [`reference`](reference.md); both are lightweight reification primitives for the external-content frontier.

The two axes are orthogonal:

| Axis | Field | Examples | Source ontology |
|---|---|---|---|
| MIME (format) | `mime:` | `text/html`, `application/pdf`, `application/json`, `text/plain` | IANA media types |
| kind (purpose) | `kind:` | `talk`, `paper`, `documentation`, `spec`, `dataset` | corpus-local |

**Each `content-types/<slug>.md` declares EXACTLY ONE axis** — populates `mime:` for a format-axis entry, populates `kind:` for a purpose-axis entry, never both.

Content-Type is a sub-subtype: a subtype of [`reference`](reference.md), which is itself a subtype of [`artifact`](artifact.md).

## Why single-axis (and why not compound)

The compound-entry approach (`paper-pdf`, `paper-html`, `paper-md`, ...) is explicitly rejected because:

- Compound entries **split backlink signal** — a query for *"show every PDF"* would have to union backlinks across every `*-pdf` compound entry instead of walking from a single `content-types/pdf.md` node
- **Combinatorial growth** — every new kind × every new MIME = a new compound entry; quickly explodes
- **Loses orthogonality** — the two axes are clean and independently queryable when each entry carries one axis

Resources combining both axes use a multi-valued `content-type:` field:

```yaml
# resources/some-paper-in-pdf.md
content-type:
  - content-types/pdf.md
  - content-types/paper.md
```

Two pointers per resource, but preserves single-axis queryability.

## When to create a content-type memorial

Reify a content-type when:

1. The corpus cites resources of that format / purpose more than once
2. The format / purpose carries metadata worth recording (canonical MIME, file extensions, structured-vs-binary axis, project-tooling parseability)
3. The reification opens useful queries (*"show every PDF the corpus cites"*, *"show every paper"*)

## When NOT to create one

- One-off rare formats (a `.bib` file cited once) — inline the format in body text
- Hyper-specific MIMEs that won't compound
- Compound combinations (`paper-pdf`) — never; multi-reference at resource level instead

