## Audit invariants

- **`content-type-has-exactly-one-axis`** (warning) — entries with both `mime:` and `kind:` populated, OR neither populated, are flagged
- **`resource-content-type-is-typed`** (warning) — resource `content-type:` field must point at `content-types/*.md` rel-paths, not literal strings

## Canonical body shape

Most content-types have **no body** — they're pure-frontmatter pointers.  When body content exists, typically:

- One paragraph: what the format / purpose is, with pointers to canonical specs (RFC 8259 for JSON, ISO 32000 for PDF, etc.)
- Project-specific notes if the format has corpus-relevant nuances (e.g., `text/html` content can be parsed via project tooling; `pdf` is opaque without external tools)

## Pitfalls

- **Declaring both axes** — flagged by audit; single-axis discipline is load-bearing
- **Compound slugs** — `paper-pdf.md` is wrong; create separate entries, multi-reference at resource level
- **Free-form `content-type:` in resources** — must point at `content-types/*.md` rel-paths, not literal strings
- **Stale extension lists** — when a MIME's accepted extensions evolve, update; authoritative source is IANA

